#exam-pointer(title: "HIGH-YIELD MEMORY HOOKS & HISTORIOGRAPHICAL POINTERS")[
  - *The Sunset Law Clause:* Payment had to reach the collectorate before sunset of the appointed date; failure triggered mandatory confiscation and public auction of the zamindari estate.
  - *Benami Transaction Artifice:* Fictitious purchases made in the name of servants, retainers, and female family members (exempt from seizure) preserved major estates like Burdwan from actual disintegration.
  - *Jotedar Synonyms across Bengal:* *Haoladars* in eastern Bengal; *Gantidars* or *Mandals* in northern Bengal; all functioned as resident village headmen wielding direct dominance over sharecroppers (*adhiyars* / *bargadars*).
  - *The Two Tools of Rajmahal:* *Hoe* (*kural*) = Paharia shifting subsistence cultivation; *Plough* = Santhal settled taxable agriculture backed by British colonial policy.
  - *Damin-i-Koh (1832):* The "skirts of the hills" demarcated with boundary pillars for Santhal settlement; transformed into the *Santhal Pargana* district (5,500 sq miles) in 1855 after the rebellion.
  - *Limitation Law of 1859:* Three-year validity of debt bonds weaponized by moneylenders to compound interest into new principal, trapping illiterate Deccan ryots in generational debt.
  - *Supa Village (Poona):* The historic spark of the May 1875 Deccan Riots, where account books (*bahikhatas*) and debt bonds were publicly burned.
]

