#cheatsheet-box(title: "MASTER STRATEGY CHEATSHEET — COLONIALISM AND RURAL SOCIETY")[
  ▸*Permanent Settlement (1793):* Enacted by Lord Cornwallis in Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa; fixed state revenue demand in perpetuity; converted rajas/taluqdars into legal landlords; strictly enforced via the Sunset Law.
  ▸*Jotedar Dominance:* Rich resident peasants who owned thousands of acres, controlled rural moneylending, rented land to sharecroppers (*adhiyars* / *bargadars*), and actively resisted absentee zamindars.
  ▸*The Fifth Report (1813):* 1,002-page British Parliamentary Select Committee report on Company misrule; exaggerated zamindari ruin to justify revoking the East India Company's trade monopoly.
  ▸*Damin-i-Koh (1832):* Forested foothill territory demarcated for Santhals to practice sedentary plough agriculture; sparked the Santhal Hul (1855–56) led by Sidhu and Kanhu against usurious *dikus*.
  ▸*Ryotwari System:* Operational in Bombay and Madras; inspired by David Ricardo's rent theory; eliminated intermediaries to assess revenue directly on individual peasants; revised every 30 years.
  ▸*Deccan Cotton Boom & Slump:* Triggered by the American Civil War (1861–65); collapsed in 1865, resulting in credit freeze, peasant bankruptcy, and the May 1875 riots at Supa (burning of *bahikhatas*).
]

#exception-box(title: "THE SEVEN FATAL CUET TRAPS")[
  1. *Trap 1 (Jotedars as Absentee Landlords):* Assuming Jotedars lived in cities. Jotedars were rich *resident* peasants living directly in villages, whereas zamindars resided in urban centers (Calcutta).
  2. *Trap 2 (Complete Collapse of Zamindaris):* Believing all Bengal zamindaris collapsed post-1793. Great zamindars survived by *benami* sales, transferring land to women, and using armed *lathyals*.
  3. *Trap 3 (Tool Symbolism Inversion):* Matching Santhals with the hoe and Paharias with the plough. Paharias used the *hoe* (shifting cultivation); Santhals used the *plough* (settled farming).
  4. *Trap 4 (Limitation Law of 1859 Interest Rate):* Assuming the law capped interest at 3%. The law capped the *legal validity of loan bonds* at 3 years, which moneylenders exploited by forcing triennial renewals.
  5. *Trap 5 (Violence Target in Deccan Riots):* Believing peasants murdered moneylenders or looted British treasuries. The rioters engaged in *symbolic violence*, solely destroying debt bonds and *bahi-khatas*.
  6. *Trap 6 (Deccan Riots Commission Exoneration):* Assuming the Commission criticized high colonial revenue rates. The report deliberately exonerated the state, blaming greedy moneylenders and peasant improvidence.
  7. *Trap 7 (Damin-i-Koh vs Santhal Pargana):* Damin-i-Koh was established in *1832* for settlement; Santhal Pargana was carved out in *1855* as a district following the suppression of the Santhal Hul.
]

